# Connect to your K3s cluster

Your K3s control plane is up and the worker nodes have joined it. The Kubernetes
API is reachable at the address below.

- **API server:** `https://{{ .Host }}:{{ .Port }}`

## Fetch your kubeconfig

Proxcloud never holds your cluster credentials — the kubeconfig lives only on the
control-plane node. SSH to the server (as the login user, with the key you
supplied at creation) and copy it out, rewriting the loopback address to the
server's real IP so you can reach the API from your workstation:

```bash
# On your workstation:
ssh proxcloud@{{ .Host }} "sudo cat /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml" \
  | sed "s/127.0.0.1/{{ .Host }}/" > ~/.kube/k3s-config

export KUBECONFIG=~/.kube/k3s-config
kubectl get nodes
```

You should see the control-plane node plus each worker in `Ready` state.

## Join secret

The cluster join token was generated for you and injected into every node at
first boot. It is **never stored, shown, or retrievable by Proxcloud** — it
exists only inside the nodes. To add a node by hand later, read the token on the
server (`sudo cat /var/lib/rancher/k3s/server/node-token`) and point a new agent
at `https://{{ .Host }}:{{ .Port }}`.

## Security note

The Kubernetes API is exposed on `{{ .Port }}/tcp` and the nodes speak the
default flannel VXLAN on `8472/udp`. Keep the cluster on a trusted LAN segment and
restrict `{{ .Port }}/tcp` with the guest firewall before running real workloads.
